 metres Freestyle, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "4630"} "4 × 200 metres Freestyle Relay, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "4596"} "100 metres Backstroke, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "4607"} "200 metres Breaststroke, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "4619"} "400 metres Breaststroke, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "4636"} "100 metres Freestyle, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "4648"} "4 × 100 metres Freestyle Relay, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "4400"} "1896"] "\n" [:option {:value "4471"} "1906"] "\n" [:option {:value "4479"} "1908"] "\n" [:option {:value "4649"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "4729"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "4829"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "4936"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "5024"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "5133"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "5241"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "5379"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "5489"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "5654"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "5850"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "6172"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "6471"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "6745"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "6962"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "7270"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "7628"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "7983"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "8352"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "8757"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "261487"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "322179"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "356997"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19000014"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2001516"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/554"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "100 metres Freestyle, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "6 – 10 July 1912"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/238"} "Simstadion, Djurgårdsbrunnsviken, Stockholm"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/4554"} "34 from 12 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The defending Olympic champion was America’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/51308"} "Charlie Daniels"] ", who had won the gold medal in this event in both " [:a {:href "/results/4471"} "1906"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/4479"} "1908"] ".  At the beginning of 1912, Daniels also held the world record, having recorded 1:02.8 over 110 yards in New York, on 15 April 1910.  However, Daniels had recently retired and did not compete at Stockholm.  The favorite’s role in Stockholm probably fell to the German " [:a {:href "/athletes/47793"} "Kurt Bretting"] ", who, on 6 April 1912, had broken Daniels’ world record with a mark of 1:02.4."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Americans were led by the little known Hawaiian " [:a {:href "/athletes/51429"} "Duke Kahanamoku"] ".  Kahanamoku had not competed in the American championships because he was so far from the mainland in an era when travel was not easy, but supposedly he had set record times in his native Hawaii."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Controversy occurred in the semi-finals, which were scheduled for the evening of 7 July, only a few hours after the quarter-finals.  For some reason, the American contingent, Kahanamoku, " [:a {:href "/athletes/51410"} "Ken Huszagh"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/51481"} "Perry McGillivray"] ", who had all qualified for the semi-finals, did not appear, thinking that the afternoon race had qualified them for the final, to be held on 9 July.  But this was not the case."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The swimming officials made allowances, however, and decided to hold an extra heat among the Americans and Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/48544"} "Mario Massa"] ", who had missed the quarter-finals due to a “misunderstanding.”  They ruled that the winner of the extra semi-final heat could advance to the final if he posted a time faster than the 3rd-place swimmer in heat one. " [:a {:href "/athletes/45217"} "Cecil Healy"] " argued on behalf of the Americans and assisted in their successful appeal to be able to swim an extra qualification race.  Kahanamoku proved his ability in this heat, and made certain that he would qualify by giving his best effort, which resulted in his equaling Bretting’s world record of 1:02.4.  Huszagh was also advanced to the final, as his mark of 1:06.2 was equal to the 3rd-place time from heat one, which had been posted by Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/45240"} "Bill Longworth"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the final, Kahanamoku took the lead early and Bretting never challenged.  Noting at the halfway mark that he had a comfortable lead, Kahanamoku eased up and still defeated Healy by almost two metres, although with a slower time than he had posted in the semi-finals."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51429"} "Duke Kahanamoku"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "1:02.6 (1 h5)"] [:td {} "1:03.8 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "1:02.4 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "1:03.4 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/45217"} "Cecil Healy"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ANZ"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ANZ.png"}] "ANZ"]] [:td {} "1:05.2 